// error: `open` marks a class whose subclasses may extend it, and a function at the top level belongs to no class: remove `open`
// error: `abstract` marks a class whose subclasses may extend it, and a function at the top level belongs to no class: remove `abstract`
// error: `override` marks a method that replaces an inherited one, and a function at the top level replaces nothing: remove it
// error: `override` marks a method that replaces an inherited one, and a class replaces nothing; remove `override`, and write `: Base` to extend a class
// error: a trait may always be implemented and extended, so it takes no `open`: remove it
// At the top level only a class is `open` or `abstract`, and nothing is `override`: each misplaced
// word is one error, and the declaration then parses as written.

open f() -> int = 1
abstract def g -> int = 2
private override h() -> int = 3

override class A
    x() -> int = 1

open trait T
    t() -> int

main() = ()
